        <div id="what-is-thatch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Thatch Is and the Half-Inch Rule
          </h2>

          <p>
            Thatch is the layer of dead and living stems, stolons, rhizomes and roots that builds up between the green grass blades and the soil surface. It is tough, fibrous material that decomposes slowly. Every lawn has some, and in moderation it is good for the grass. A thin layer insulates the soil against temperature swings, cushions the crowns of the grass plants against foot traffic, and slows evaporation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problems start when it gets thick. Beyond about half an inch, thatch behaves like a sponge that is hard to wet and quick to dry. Rain and irrigation soak into it and stop there instead of reaching the soil. Fertilizer gets tied up in it. Roots, following the moisture, begin growing into the thatch itself instead of down into the soil, and a lawn rooted in thatch is shallow, drought-prone and easily damaged by heat and cold. Thick thatch also harbors insects and disease.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why the half-inch threshold is the standard guidance. Under half an inch, leave it alone. Around half an inch to an inch, a dethatcher is worth using. Over an inch, you likely need a more aggressive approach, such as a rented power rake or verticutter, and possibly a staged plan over two seasons.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to measure it</h3>

          <p>
            Use a trowel or a knife to cut a small wedge of turf a few inches deep and lift it out. Look at the side. You will see green grass at the top, soil at the bottom, and between them a brown, spongy layer of tangled material. Measure that layer. Do this in several spots, because thatch is often worse in sunny areas, heavily fertilized areas and parts of the lawn with vigorous creeping grasses. A lawn that feels springy underfoot is a hint, but the wedge tells you for certain.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE HALF-INCH RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Under 1/2 inch, leave it. Over 1/2 inch, dethatch. Over 1 inch, plan a staged job.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Measure before you buy anything. Dethatching a lawn that does not need it just stresses healthy grass and gives weeds an opening.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

