        <div id="dethatcher-vs-scarifier" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Dethatcher vs Scarifier
          </h2>

          <p>
            The words get used interchangeably on product listings, but the tools do different things, and choosing the right one for your lawn matters.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dethatcher: spring tines</h3>

          <p>
            A dethatcher uses a reel of flexible spring steel tines that spin and comb through the grass. They catch loose dead material, moss and debris near the surface and flick it up and out. Because the tines flex, the action is relatively gentle: it removes the loose top of the thatch layer without cutting much living grass. It is the right choice for routine maintenance, for lawns with thatch around half an inch, and for a spring cleanup after winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scarifier or verticutter: fixed blades</h3>

          <p>
            A scarifier uses a reel of fixed, rigid blades that slice vertically down through the thatch and a short way into the soil. It cuts through matted thatch that spring tines would skate over, it cuts the runners of creeping grasses, and it scores the soil surface. That last point is why scarifying is so useful before overseeding: the grooves give seed contact with soil. The downside is stress. Scarifying removes more material and cuts more living grass, so the lawn looks rough for a few weeks and needs good growing conditions to recover.
          </p>

          <p>
            A verticutter is essentially a heavier-duty scarifier, often a gas-powered rental with more widely spaced blades set to cut into the soil. A power rake is sometimes used for the same machine and sometimes for a heavy flail-type dethatcher; ask the rental store what the blades look like.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most homeowners, a 2-in-1 electric machine covers both needs. Use the dethatching reel for a light pass in spring or for maintenance, and switch to the scarifier in early fall on cool-season grass when you plan to overseed. If your thatch is over an inch, rent a verticutter rather than asking a homeowner machine to do it.
          </p>

