          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Electric vs Tow-Behind vs Manual Rake
          </h2>

          <p>
            The right kind of dethatcher depends mostly on lawn size, and to a lesser degree on what you already own.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Corded electric</h3>

          <p>
            Corded walk-behind dethatchers suit most suburban lawns, up to roughly a quarter acre. They are light, cheap to run, need no maintenance beyond occasional tine replacement, and the 2-in-1 models scarify as well. The only real downside is the cord. Use a heavy-gauge outdoor extension cord of the length the manual specifies, plan your pattern to work away from the outlet, and keep the cord behind you on the finished side. The Greenworks 27022 and the Sun Joe pair are the models to consider.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cordless</h3>

          <p>
            A battery machine like the Greenworks 40V removes the cord problem and the trip hazard. Runtime is the constraint, so check it against your lawn size. It makes the most sense if you already own batteries from the same system, because a kit with battery and charger costs substantially more than a corded machine.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tow-behind</h3>

          <p>
            For half an acre and up, a tow-behind spring-tine dethatcher behind a lawn tractor or zero-turn is the only sensible option. It covers ground many times faster than a walk-behind. Understand the trade-off: these are power-less rakes, so they comb thatch up but do not slice or collect it. Add weight for deeper action, then pick up the debris with a bagging mower, a lawn sweeper or a blower.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Manual thatch rake</h3>

          <p>
            For a small lawn, a strip along a path or a few patches, a good thatch rake does the job without a machine to store. It is also the tool for edges and corners after a machine pass.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Renting</h3>

          <p>
            If your thatch is over an inch or you only plan to dethatch once, rent a gas power rake or verticutter for a day. It is heavier, more aggressive and faster than homeowner machines, and it is the right tool for a lawn that has been neglected for years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When to Dethatch
          </h2>

          <p>
            Dethatching is stressful for grass, so the rule is the same one that applies to aeration: do it when the grass is growing strongly and has several weeks of good weather to recover. Dethatching at the wrong time is one of the most common ways people damage a lawn they were trying to help.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cool-season grass</h3>

          <p>
            For Kentucky bluegrass, fine fescues, tall fescue and perennial ryegrass, early fall is the best time, around late August through September across much of the northern US. The grass is growing vigorously in cool weather, the soil is still warm enough for overseeding, and weeds are slowing down. Early spring is the second choice, once the lawn has greened up and been mowed once or twice, but keep a spring pass light. A hard spring dethatch opens the ground just as crabgrass is germinating and leaves the lawn little time to recover before summer heat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Warm-season grass</h3>

          <p>
            Bermudagrass, zoysia and St. Augustine should be dethatched from late spring into early summer, after the lawn has fully greened up and is growing actively. These grasses spread by stolons and rhizomes and recover fast in heat, which also makes them prone to thatch. Do not dethatch them in fall, when they are slowing toward dormancy, or in early spring before they have fully woken up.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever grass you have, avoid dethatching during drought, in extreme heat, or when the lawn is dormant. The soil should be slightly moist, not wet. Dry thatch is tough and the machine skates over it; wet ground lets tines tear out living grass by the roots. Watering lightly a day or two before usually gets conditions right.
          </p>

