        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Measure before you dethatch. If the thatch is under half an inch, leave it, aerate if the soil is hard, and adjust your feeding and watering. If it is over half an inch, a corded machine like the Greenworks 27022 handles most suburban lawns, a Sun Joe 2-in-1 adds a scarifier for overseeding, and a Brinly DT-48BH behind a tractor makes short work of a big lawn.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever you use, do it when your grass is growing hardest, start shallow, clean up everything the machine pulls, and put seed and fertilizer down straight away. Then change the habits that built the thatch, and you should not need to do it again for years.
          </p>
